## The Adapter Pattern – A “Bridge” to a New Interface

### 1.  What It Is

The **Adapter** (also called **Wrapper**) pattern lets you use an existing class (the *Adaptee*) where a different interface (the *Target*) is expected.  
It’s a *structural* pattern – it deals with how classes and objects are combined – not with what they *do*.

> **When to use it**  
> • You have legacy code that you cannot change.  
> • You need to integrate it with new code that expects a different interface.  
> • You want to keep the existing class untouched but expose its functionality through a new API.

### 2.  Key Roles

| Role | Responsibility |
|------|----------------|
| **Target** | The interface that the client expects to use. |
| **Client** | Uses the Target interface; it doesn’t know the Adapter. |
| **Adaptee** | Existing class with an incompatible interface. |
| **Adapter** | Implements Target and holds (or extends) an Adaptee. It translates calls from Target to Adaptee. |

### 3.  The Classic “Media Player” Example

The most often‑cited example shows an old `MediaPlayer` interface that can play MP3s, and a new `AdvancedMediaPlayer` that can play MP4s and DVDs.  
The `MediaAdapter` lets the old interface play the new formats.

```
┌───────────────────────┐
│     Target (MediaPlayer)     │
│   + play(String type, String filename)   │
└───────────────────────┘
          ▲
          │
          │ implements
          ▼
┌───────────────────────┐
│     Adapter (MediaAdapter)   │
│   - advancedPlayer : AdvancedMediaPlayer   │
│   + play(...) { … }   │
└───────────────────────┘
          ▲
          │ uses
          ▼
┌───────────────────────┐
│     Adaptee (AdvancedMediaPlayerImpl) │
│   + playVCD(String)   │
│   + playDVD(String)   │
└───────────────────────┘
```

### 4.  Java Code Example

Below is a minimal, self‑contained implementation.  
Feel free to copy‑paste into a file called `MediaAdapterDemo.java` and run it.

```java
// ---------- Target ----------
interface MediaPlayer {
    void play(String audioType, String fileName);
}

// ---------- Adaptee ----------
interface AdvancedMediaPlayer {
    void playVCD(String fileName);
    void playDVD(String fileName);
}

class VCDPlayer implements AdvancedMediaPlayer {
    @Override public void playVCD(String fileName) {
        System.out.println("Playing VCD: " + fileName);
    }
    @Override public void playDVD(String fileName) {
        // Not supported
    }
}

class DVDPlayer implements AdvancedMediaPlayer {
    @Override public void playVCD(String fileName) {
        // Not supported
    }
    @Override public void playDVD(String fileName) {
        System.out.println("Playing DVD: " + fileName);
    }
}

// ---------- Adapter ----------
class MediaAdapter implements MediaPlayer {

    // The adaptee – we delegate to this object
    private AdvancedMediaPlayer advancedPlayer;

    public MediaAdapter(String audioType) {
        // Decide which concrete Adaptee to use
        if (audioType.equalsIgnoreCase("VCD")) {
            advancedPlayer = new VCDPlayer();
        } else if (audioType.equalsIgnoreCase("DVD")) {
            advancedPlayer = new DVDPlayer();
        } else {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unsupported format: " + audioType);
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void play(String audioType, String fileName) {
        // Translate the call
        if (audioType.equalsIgnoreCase("VCD")) {
            advancedPlayer.playVCD(fileName);
        } else if (audioType.equalsIgnoreCase("DVD")) {
            advancedPlayer.playDVD(fileName);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Audio type " + audioType + " not supported by adapter");
        }
    }
}

// ---------- Client ----------
class AudioPlayer implements MediaPlayer {

    // The client only knows the Target interface
    @Override
    public void play(String audioType, String fileName) {
        // If it can play MP3 directly, do it
        if (audioType.equalsIgnoreCase("MP3")) {
            System.out.println("Playing MP3 file. Name = " + fileName);
        }
        // Otherwise delegate to the adapter
        else if (audioType.equalsIgnoreCase("VCD")
                || audioType.equalsIgnoreCase("DVD")) {
            MediaAdapter adapter = new MediaAdapter(audioType);
            adapter.play(audioType, fileName);
        }
        else {
            System.out.println("Invalid media. " + audioType + " not supported");
        }
    }
}

// ---------- Demo ----------
public class MediaAdapterDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AudioPlayer audioPlayer = new AudioPlayer();

        audioPlayer.play("MP3", "song.mp3");
        audioPlayer.play("VCD", "movie.vcd");
        audioPlayer.play("DVD", "movie.dvd");
        audioPlayer.play("MPEG", "movie.mpeg");
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
Playing MP3 file. Name = song.mp3
Playing VCD: movie.vcd
Playing DVD: movie.dvd
Invalid media. MPEG not supported
```

### 5.  Why This Works

| What the Client Wants | What the Adaptee Can Do |
|------------------------|------------------------|
| `MediaPlayer.play("VCD", ...)` | `AdvancedMediaPlayer.playVCD(...)` |
| `MediaPlayer.play("DVD", ...)` | `AdvancedMediaPlayer.playDVD(...)` |

The **Adapter** translates the client’s call into the appropriate call on the `AdvancedMediaPlayer` implementation.  
The client remains oblivious to the fact that a different object is handling the work.

### 6.  Variations

| Type | How It Works | When To Choose |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **Class Adapter** | Adapter extends the Adaptee (Java only allows single inheritance, so this works only if Adaptee is a concrete class) | Rare in Java, more common in languages that support multiple inheritance |
| **Object Adapter** | Adapter contains an instance of the Adaptee (composition) | Most common in Java (shown above) |
| **Interface Adapter** | Adapter implements an interface but leaves methods empty (useful when you only need to override a subset) | When only part of an interface is needed |

### 7.  Real‑World Analogy

Think of the Adapter like a **power plug adapter**:  

- The **Target** is the socket in your house (expects a 110 V, 50 Hz plug).  
- The **Adaptee** is the appliance’s plug (maybe a 220 V, 60 Hz plug).  
- The **Adapter** is the little device that physically and electrically converts the plug to fit the socket.  
You never have to modify the appliance (Adaptee) or the house (Target); you just add the Adapter.

### 8.  Summary

- **Adapter** = *“I will let you use this old class in a new context by translating calls.”*  
- **Target** = the interface the client expects.  
- **Adaptee** = the existing class with an incompatible interface.  
- **Adapter** = implements Target, contains an Adaptee, forwards/ translates calls.  
- **Benefit** = reuse legacy code, add new functionality without breaking existing clients, keep codebase clean.

Feel free to experiment by adding more media types or by turning the `MediaAdapter` into an *object* adapter that can be reused for multiple client calls. Happy coding!
